1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,809
Welcome to episode twelve.

2
00:00:01,849 --> 00:00:05,650
"Which fuel is better?" sounds
like a simple question.

3
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:11,482
Today we'll see that the honest answer
always starts with "better on what basis?",

4
00:00:11,522 --> 00:00:16,034
and that sustainability needs
evidence from the whole lifecycle,

5
00:00:16,074 --> 00:00:18,279
not just the moment of burning.

6
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:19,874
Two quick checks.

7
00:00:19,914 --> 00:00:22,515
Yeast enzymes catalyse fermentation.

8
00:00:22,555 --> 00:00:25,669
Do they change the
delta H of the reaction?

9
00:00:25,709 --> 00:00:31,244
And a heater with twenty-five percent
efficiency must deliver ten point zero

10
00:00:31,284 --> 00:00:33,151
megajoules of useful heat.

11
00:00:33,191 --> 00:00:35,425
How much fuel energy is needed?

12
00:00:35,465 --> 00:00:36,712
Pause and answer.

13
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:49,707
Enzymes lower the activation
energy but don't change delta H.

14
00:00:49,747 --> 00:00:55,455
And the fuel energy needed is ten point
zero divided by zero point two five:

15
00:00:55,495 --> 00:00:57,573
forty point zero megajoules.

16
00:00:57,613 --> 00:01:00,336
A lower efficiency means more input.

17
00:01:01,333 --> 00:01:03,670
Let's compare methane and octane.

18
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:09,289
Burning one mole of octane releases about
five thousand four hundred and sixty

19
00:01:09,329 --> 00:01:10,081
kilojoules.

20
00:01:10,121 --> 00:01:13,971
One mole of methane releases
eight hundred and ninety.

21
00:01:14,011 --> 00:01:19,846
Per mole, octane wins by a long way,
because each octane molecule is much bigger.

22
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,101
Now per gram.

23
00:01:21,141 --> 00:01:27,113
Methane: eight hundred and ninety divided
by sixteen point zero is about fifty-five

24
00:01:27,153 --> 00:01:29,286
point six kilojoules per gram.

25
00:01:29,326 --> 00:01:35,080
Octane: five thousand four hundred and
sixty divided by one hundred and fourteen

26
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:38,195
point zero is about
forty-seven point nine.

27
00:01:38,235 --> 00:01:39,900
Per gram, methane wins.

28
00:01:40,266 --> 00:01:42,277
Neither statement is wrong.

29
00:01:42,317 --> 00:01:44,708
They answer different questions.

30
00:01:44,748 --> 00:01:50,328
And for a real heating task there's a
third basis that often matters most:

31
00:01:50,368 --> 00:01:53,139
how much fuel, and how much emission,

32
00:01:53,179 --> 00:01:57,392
does it take to deliver a
given amount of useful energy?

33
00:01:57,432 --> 00:01:59,787
That depends on efficiency too.

34
00:02:00,733 --> 00:02:06,097
Here's another basis that matters
for gaseous fuels: energy per litre.

35
00:02:06,137 --> 00:02:11,116
Hydrogen releases two hundred and
eighty-six kilojoules per mole,

36
00:02:11,156 --> 00:02:15,517
and its molar mass is only
two point zero grams per mole.

37
00:02:15,557 --> 00:02:21,925
Methane releases eight hundred and ninety,
with a molar mass of sixteen point zero.

38
00:02:21,965 --> 00:02:26,557
Which is better per gram, and
which per litre of gas at SLC?

39
00:02:26,597 --> 00:02:28,296
Pause and work it out.

40
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:42,061
Per gram, hydrogen wins easily: two
hundred and eighty-six divided by two point

41
00:02:42,101 --> 00:02:45,866
zero is one hundred and
forty-three kilojoules per gram,

42
00:02:45,906 --> 00:02:49,507
compared with about fifty-five
point six for methane.

43
00:02:49,866 --> 00:02:54,579
But a mole of any gas at SLC occupies
twenty-four point eight litres.

44
00:02:54,619 --> 00:02:59,952
So per litre, hydrogen gives two hundred
and eighty-six divided by twenty-four

45
00:02:59,992 --> 00:03:03,258
point eight, about eleven
point five kilojoules,

46
00:03:03,298 --> 00:03:08,768
while methane gives eight hundred and
ninety divided by twenty-four point eight,

47
00:03:08,808 --> 00:03:10,766
about thirty-five point nine.

48
00:03:10,806 --> 00:03:13,865
Per litre, methane wins
by about three times.

49
00:03:13,905 --> 00:03:18,274
That's why storing enough hydrogen
for a vehicle is a challenge,

50
00:03:18,314 --> 00:03:20,793
even though it's excellent per gram.

51
00:03:21,732 --> 00:03:25,063
Here's a comparison that
shows an important idea.

52
00:03:25,103 --> 00:03:28,641
Ethanol and propane have
almost the same molar mass:

53
00:03:28,681 --> 00:03:32,907
forty-six point zero and forty-four
point zero grams per mole.

54
00:03:32,947 --> 00:03:38,204
But propane releases two thousand two
hundred and twenty kilojoules per mole,

55
00:03:38,244 --> 00:03:42,057
and ethanol only one thousand
three hundred and seventy.

56
00:03:42,097 --> 00:03:47,424
Per gram, that's about fifty point five
kilojoules for propane and twenty-nine

57
00:03:47,464 --> 00:03:49,115
point eight for ethanol.

58
00:03:49,465 --> 00:03:50,699
Why so different?

59
00:03:50,739 --> 00:03:55,344
Ethanol already contains an oxygen
atom, in its hydroxy group.

60
00:03:55,384 --> 00:03:58,415
Part of the molecule is already oxidised,

61
00:03:58,455 --> 00:04:04,074
so there's less left to oxidise when
it burns, and less energy is released.

62
00:04:04,465 --> 00:04:06,550
That's the explanation to give.

63
00:04:06,590 --> 00:04:11,690
Saying only that the two fuels have
different bond enthalpies isn't enough:

64
00:04:11,730 --> 00:04:17,446
a bond-enthalpy argument would need full
calculations for both combustion equations.

65
00:04:17,486 --> 00:04:22,860
The same idea explains why methanol
releases less energy per gram than ethanol:

66
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,641
a bigger fraction of its mass is oxygen.

67
00:04:26,598 --> 00:04:29,008
Here's the fair way to compare.

68
00:04:29,048 --> 00:04:34,778
Fix the task first: deliver one point
zero zero megajoule of useful heat.

69
00:04:34,818 --> 00:04:37,663
Then, for each fuel, work backwards.

70
00:04:37,998 --> 00:04:42,264
Fuel energy input equals useful
output divided by efficiency.

71
00:04:42,304 --> 00:04:47,417
Mass of fuel equals that input divided
by the fuel's energy per kilogram.

72
00:04:47,457 --> 00:04:51,370
And if emissions are given
per megajoule of fuel energy,

73
00:04:51,410 --> 00:04:55,504
then emissions equal the input
times that emission factor.

74
00:04:55,865 --> 00:05:01,196
Watch the units: megajoules divided by
megajoules per kilogram gives kilograms.

75
00:05:01,236 --> 00:05:04,528
Megajoules times grams
per megajoule gives grams.

76
00:05:04,568 --> 00:05:08,171
Now both fuels are compared
on exactly the same task.

77
00:05:09,131 --> 00:05:13,144
Practice question Q twenty-three
is worth seven marks.

78
00:05:13,184 --> 00:05:17,121
The data are hypothetical,
made up for this exercise.

79
00:05:17,161 --> 00:05:19,188
Pause the video and try it.

80
00:05:34,597 --> 00:05:38,720
Inputs for one point zero zero
megajoule of useful heat.

81
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:41,693
Fuel R is thirty-five percent efficient:

82
00:05:41,733 --> 00:05:47,714
one point zero zero divided by zero point
three five zero is two point eight five

83
00:05:47,754 --> 00:05:48,977
seven megajoules.

84
00:05:49,017 --> 00:05:52,248
Biofuel S is twenty-eight
percent efficient:

85
00:05:52,288 --> 00:05:58,343
one point zero zero divided by zero point
two eight zero is three point five seven

86
00:05:58,383 --> 00:05:59,497
one megajoules.

87
00:05:59,864 --> 00:06:00,353
Masses.

88
00:06:00,393 --> 00:06:05,949
R: two point eight five seven megajoules
divided by forty-three point zero

89
00:06:05,989 --> 00:06:10,940
megajoules per kilogram is zero
point zero six six four kilograms.

90
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:17,065
S: three point five seven one divided by
twenty-nine point zero is zero point one

91
00:06:17,105 --> 00:06:18,577
two three kilograms.

92
00:06:18,617 --> 00:06:19,979
R needs less mass.

93
00:06:20,331 --> 00:06:21,706
Lifecycle emissions.

94
00:06:21,746 --> 00:06:26,943
R: two point eight five seven megajoules
times eighty point zero grams per

95
00:06:26,983 --> 00:06:32,746
megajoule is about two hundred and twenty-nine
grams of carbon dioxide equivalent.

96
00:06:32,786 --> 00:06:38,549
S: three point five seven one times forty-five
point zero is about one hundred and

97
00:06:38,589 --> 00:06:39,682
sixty-one grams.

98
00:06:39,722 --> 00:06:43,756
S has lower lifecycle emissions
for the same useful heat.

99
00:06:44,131 --> 00:06:49,998
So the claim that lower fuel mass must
mean lower emissions is false here.

100
00:06:50,038 --> 00:06:55,746
R uses less mass, but S emits less
over its lifecycle for the same task.

101
00:06:55,786 --> 00:06:59,258
Mass and emissions
measure different things.

102
00:06:59,298 --> 00:07:04,766
Marks: two for the inputs, two for
the masses, two for the emissions,

103
00:07:04,806 --> 00:07:06,962
and one for the evaluation.

104
00:07:07,930 --> 00:07:08,686
Checkpoint.

105
00:07:08,726 --> 00:07:11,360
Imagine another hypothetical fuel, T,

106
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,480
with lower emissions per
megajoule of fuel energy than R:

107
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:17,793
seventy grams instead of eighty.

108
00:07:17,833 --> 00:07:22,492
But it's burned in a heater that's
only twenty percent efficient.

109
00:07:22,532 --> 00:07:26,828
Which fuel has lower emissions
per megajoule of useful heat?

110
00:07:26,868 --> 00:07:28,459
Pause and work it out.

111
00:07:36,864 --> 00:07:43,085
For T: seventy divided by zero point two
zero is three hundred and fifty grams per

112
00:07:43,125 --> 00:07:44,382
useful megajoule.

113
00:07:44,422 --> 00:07:48,811
For R, we already found about
two hundred and twenty-nine.

114
00:07:48,851 --> 00:07:54,843
So R is lower per useful output, even
though T looked better per unit of input.

115
00:07:54,883 --> 00:08:00,874
A lower emission per unit of input energy
doesn't guarantee lower emissions per

116
00:08:00,914 --> 00:08:04,427
unit of useful output;
efficiency matters too.

117
00:08:05,397 --> 00:08:08,577
Where you draw the boundary
changes the answer.

118
00:08:08,617 --> 00:08:13,578
Draw a tight boundary around just the
burning, and you count operational,

119
00:08:13,618 --> 00:08:17,523
or direct, emissions: the
carbon dioxide from combustion.

120
00:08:17,863 --> 00:08:18,702
Now widen it.

121
00:08:18,742 --> 00:08:22,421
Growing the feedstock may
use fertiliser and machinery.

122
00:08:22,461 --> 00:08:24,923
Processing needs heat or electricity.

123
00:08:24,963 --> 00:08:27,965
The fuel must be
transported and distributed.

124
00:08:28,005 --> 00:08:32,360
All of these can produce emissions,
and together with combustion,

125
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:34,901
they make up the lifecycle emissions.

126
00:08:35,263 --> 00:08:37,673
Methane deserves a special mention.

127
00:08:37,713 --> 00:08:42,712
Biogas and natural gas systems can
leak unburnt methane, from digesters,

128
00:08:42,752 --> 00:08:43,762
pipes or wells.

129
00:08:43,802 --> 00:08:48,311
Methane is a much more potent
greenhouse gas than carbon dioxide,

130
00:08:48,351 --> 00:08:51,670
so even small leaks can
outweigh other benefits.

131
00:08:51,710 --> 00:08:56,259
A claim about emissions should
always say which boundary it uses.

132
00:08:57,196 --> 00:08:57,997
Checkpoint.

133
00:08:58,037 --> 00:09:01,438
A company says its
biofuel is carbon neutral,

134
00:09:01,478 --> 00:09:07,555
because the carbon dioxide released when
it burns was absorbed by the crop as it

135
00:09:07,595 --> 00:09:07,937
grew.

136
00:09:07,977 --> 00:09:10,231
Is that claim fully justified?

137
00:09:10,271 --> 00:09:11,571
Pause and decide.

138
00:09:19,996 --> 00:09:21,426
Part of it is right.

139
00:09:21,466 --> 00:09:27,379
The carbon in a biofuel was recently taken
from the atmosphere by photosynthesis,

140
00:09:27,419 --> 00:09:33,038
so burning it returns carbon that was
already in the short-term carbon cycle,

141
00:09:33,078 --> 00:09:38,149
unlike fossil carbon that had been
locked away for millions of years.

142
00:09:38,529 --> 00:09:41,318
But the whole lifecycle usually adds more.

143
00:09:41,358 --> 00:09:43,878
Fertiliser production, farm machinery,

144
00:09:43,918 --> 00:09:47,515
processing heat and transport
often use fossil energy,

145
00:09:47,555 --> 00:09:49,536
and some systems leak methane.

146
00:09:49,576 --> 00:09:53,645
So a biofuel can have lower net
emissions than a fossil fuel,

147
00:09:53,685 --> 00:09:56,743
but "carbon neutral"
needs lifecycle evidence.

148
00:09:56,783 --> 00:10:00,353
Without it, "lower-carbon"
is the most you can claim.

149
00:10:01,329 --> 00:10:05,746
A renewable feedstock regrows,
but growing it still has costs.

150
00:10:05,786 --> 00:10:11,712
A fuel crop can compete with food crops
for land, use large amounts of fresh water,

151
00:10:11,752 --> 00:10:13,797
and replace natural habitats.

152
00:10:13,837 --> 00:10:18,007
So a renewable fuel isn't
automatically a sustainable one.

153
00:10:18,395 --> 00:10:21,550
Using wastes avoids some
of these trade-offs.

154
00:10:21,590 --> 00:10:25,738
Here's a circular example: food
waste goes into a digester,

155
00:10:25,778 --> 00:10:31,205
which produces biogas for heat; the
leftover digestate becomes fertiliser for

156
00:10:31,245 --> 00:10:35,890
crops; the crops become food, and
food waste returns to the start.

157
00:10:35,930 --> 00:10:39,338
Materials stay in use
instead of becoming waste.

158
00:10:39,729 --> 00:10:42,662
This connects to green
chemistry principles.

159
00:10:42,702 --> 00:10:46,717
Using renewable feedstocks, such
as plant material or waste.

160
00:10:46,757 --> 00:10:50,298
Preventing waste, by turning
by-products into inputs.

161
00:10:50,338 --> 00:10:52,461
Designing for energy efficiency.

162
00:10:52,501 --> 00:10:56,110
And using catalysts, like
the enzymes in fermentation,

163
00:10:56,150 --> 00:10:59,218
to make processes run
under milder conditions.

164
00:10:59,258 --> 00:11:01,584
And aiming for a high atom economy,

165
00:11:01,624 --> 00:11:06,854
so that more of the reactant atoms end
up in the useful product rather than in

166
00:11:06,894 --> 00:11:07,705
by-products.

167
00:11:08,062 --> 00:11:11,781
It also links to the United Nations
Sustainable Development Goals:

168
00:11:11,821 --> 00:11:15,655
affordable and clean energy,
responsible consumption and production,

169
00:11:15,695 --> 00:11:20,422
climate action, and zero hunger, which is
where food-versus-fuel trade-offs matter.

170
00:11:21,394 --> 00:11:25,119
Practice question Q
twenty-four is worth six marks.

171
00:11:25,159 --> 00:11:30,030
Pause the video and plan your answer
before we build one together.

172
00:11:45,461 --> 00:11:47,521
First consideration: feedstock.

173
00:11:47,561 --> 00:11:52,533
Process B uses food-processing waste,
while Process A uses an edible crop.

174
00:11:52,573 --> 00:11:57,748
So B avoids competing with food for land
and makes productive use of a waste,

175
00:11:57,788 --> 00:12:03,031
which fits the green chemistry principle
of using renewable feedstocks and the

176
00:12:03,071 --> 00:12:06,457
circular-economy idea of
keeping materials in use.

177
00:12:06,794 --> 00:12:09,162
Second consideration: resource use.

178
00:12:09,202 --> 00:12:12,670
Per kilogram of ethanol,
A uses less listed energy,

179
00:12:12,710 --> 00:12:16,590
eight megajoules compared with
ten, and less fresh water,

180
00:12:16,630 --> 00:12:18,997
twelve litres compared with twenty.

181
00:12:19,037 --> 00:12:21,170
On those measures, A is better.

182
00:12:21,528 --> 00:12:27,921
So there's a trade-off: B is better on
feedstock and on using its carbon dioxide;

183
00:12:27,961 --> 00:12:30,462
A is better on energy and water.

184
00:12:30,502 --> 00:12:36,895
Also note that capturing carbon dioxide
and using it in another process isn't the

185
00:12:36,935 --> 00:12:41,462
same as storing it permanently;
it may be released later.

186
00:12:41,861 --> 00:12:45,587
Can we say which has lower
total lifecycle emissions?

187
00:12:45,627 --> 00:12:45,800
No.

188
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:50,988
A uses natural-gas heat, and B uses
electricity that's only sixty percent

189
00:12:51,028 --> 00:12:55,180
renewable, and we aren't given
emission factors for either,

190
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:58,449
or data on transport and
feedstock production.

191
00:12:58,489 --> 00:13:01,616
Stating that limit
honestly is worth a mark.

192
00:13:01,994 --> 00:13:05,575
A recommendation must follow
from stated priorities.

193
00:13:05,615 --> 00:13:11,075
For example: if avoiding competition with
food is the priority, B is preferred,

194
00:13:11,115 --> 00:13:13,860
despite its higher energy and water use.

195
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:19,291
Either process can be defended, as long
as the reasoning matches the evidence.

196
00:13:19,331 --> 00:13:23,050
Marks: two plus two for the
two supported comparisons,

197
00:13:23,090 --> 00:13:28,730
one for the trade-off or limit, and one
for a consistent, conditional conclusion.

198
00:13:29,727 --> 00:13:30,355
To recap.

199
00:13:30,395 --> 00:13:36,509
State the basis before you compare fuels:
per mole, per gram, or per useful energy.

200
00:13:36,549 --> 00:13:41,626
To compare emissions for a task,
normalise to the same useful output,

201
00:13:41,666 --> 00:13:43,628
which brings in efficiency.

202
00:13:43,668 --> 00:13:46,742
Say which boundary an emission claim uses.

203
00:13:46,782 --> 00:13:51,083
And remember that renewable
isn't the same as sustainable.

204
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:52,458
Closing recall.

205
00:13:52,498 --> 00:13:56,786
Name two reasons a renewable
biofuel might not be sustainable.

206
00:14:07,194 --> 00:14:12,702
For example: its crop competes with
food for land or uses a lot of water,

207
00:14:12,742 --> 00:14:18,478
or methane leaks from its production,
or processing relies on fossil energy.

208
00:14:18,518 --> 00:14:23,458
Next episode is our first exam
workshop, on fuels and combustion.
